-Instrumented runs have profiling overhead; compare final uninstrumented timings for the speed target. After the shared-setup pass, the largest remaining groups by RSpec time are `Trends::Statuses`, `Status::ThreadingConcern`, and `MediaAttachment`. Request specs still need a separate baseline and optimization pass.
+Instrumented runs have profiling overhead; compare final uninstrumented timings for the speed target. After the shared-setup pass, the largest remaining model groups by RSpec time are `Trends::Statuses`, `Status::ThreadingConcern`, and `MediaAttachment`. Request results are recorded below.
+
+## Request spec baseline and optimization (2026-11-23)
+
+Command:
+
+```shell
+bundle exec rspec spec/requests --format progress --profile 30 --seed 12533
+```
+
+| Run | Examples | RSpec time | Wall time |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Baseline, requests use inline Sidekiq | 1877 | 269.0s | 272s |
+| Fake Sidekiq, shared request setup, warn-level logging | 1877 | 100.9s | 103s |
+
+Both runs completed with zero failures. The request suite is 62.5% faster by RSpec time and 62.1% faster by wall time. Together with the model optimization, the original 445.9s combined RSpec time is now 200.21s (2.23x faster); combined wall time is 452.361s versus 205.925s (2.20x faster).
+
+## Request profiling findings
+
+- EventProf measured 7,592 inline Sidekiq events taking 97.617s, 32.12% of the instrumented run. SQL notifications measured 237,901 events taking 55.342s. Instrumented runs took 303.937s, so these values include profiling overhead and are not used for the speed comparison.
+- Request specs now use Sidekiq's fake mode by default. Examples and scopes that require delivered mail or synchronous feed/notification/work side effects opt into `sidekiq: :inline`. Notification fixture graphs are executed inline once in `before_all` and immediately restore fake mode.
+- RSpec Dissect initially measured 92.369s of setup in a 144.823s run. Repeated fixture matrices and service-created graphs in caching, timelines, ActivityPub, notifications, account feeds, reports, and status activity now use `let_it_be`/`before_all` with `refind: true` where records are mutable.
+- The shared API authentication context now shares its base user and default token per group. A fresh token is still fabricated when a nested example changes scopes or changes the authenticated user, so scope/role coverage remains isolated.
+- Test logging runs at warn level so request specs do not format and write the hundreds of thousands of DEBUG SQL entries. A prior request run rotated more than 100 MB of test logs. Redis teardown uses `flushdb`, equivalent to the previous `KEYS` plus delete-all cleanup but with one command per example.
+- Factory definitions and application runtime behavior were not changed.
+
+## Reprofiling request specs
+
+Profile repeated setup:
+
+```shell
+RD_PROF=1 RD_PROF_TOP=40 RD_PROF_LET_TOP=8 \
+  bundle exec rspec spec/requests --require ./spec/rails_helper --require test_prof \
+  --format progress --seed 12533
+```
+
+Profile SQL and Sidekiq events:
+
+```shell
+EVENT_PROF='sql.active_record,sidekiq.inline' \
+  EVENT_PROF_EXAMPLES=1 EVENT_PROF_TOP=30 \
+  bundle exec rspec spec/requests --require ./spec/rails_helper --require test_prof \
+  --format progress --seed 12533
+```
+
+The remaining slow request groups after this pass are intentionally behavior-heavy: signature verification, large ActivityPub pagination fixtures, and media processing. Keep future comparisons uninstrumented and scoped to `spec/requests`; system and streaming suites remain excluded.
